The post comes after Underwood was booed by audience members during Monday&#8217;s &#8220;Hollywood Week in Music City &#8211; Part 2&#8221; episode. The country star received negative feedback from the crowd after critiquing a contestant named Mor&#8217;s choice to perform an original song . The &#8220;Before He Cheats&#8221; singer knew what was coming before she made her comments.

&#8220;I feel you guys are gonna boo me,” she said to the audience. Once the booing started, she turned toward the crowd and said, &#8220;You&#8217;re not included in the discussion.&#8221;

Fellow country singer and American Idol judge Luke Bryan attempted to defend Underwood by reminding the audience, &#8220;She only won this. She knows,&#8221; referring to the star&#8217;s season four win. Two decades after her victory, Underwood replaced Katy Perry on the judges panel last year when the &#8220;Firework&#8221; singer departed American Idol after seven seasons.

The &#8220;Jesus, Take the Wheel&#8221; singer is no stranger to negative feedback. Just two months before she joined American Idol in 2025, Underwood took the stage at President Donald Trump&#8217;s inauguration to sing &#8220;America the Beautiful.&#8221; She received backlash for the performance from the president&#8217;s opponents and disappointed some fans of hers. One X fan page, formerly known as Carrie Underwood Charts, rebranded as a Megan Moroney account and commented that Underwood &#8220;disappointed the entire generation z.&#8221;

Unlike with Monday&#8217;s boos, Underwood has remained mostly silent on the backlash from last year, only telling The Independent before the ceremony that she &#8220;[loves] our country and [was] honored to have been asked to sing at the inauguration.&#8221;

The next episode of American Idol airs on Monday March 9 at 8 p.m. on ABC and will be available for streaming the next day on Hulu .
